The skill’s decision rule makes the JSON evidence—not just the supplied scan summary—the basis for the refactor. I’m now checking the file-specific context and the exact JSX so the proposal preserves the component’s behavior.

The global `crimes` binary is not installed in this shell. I found the fixture source and will use the repository’s locally built CLI, as the project instructions prescribe; I’m also checking the fixture’s Tailwind theme so token names in the proposal are grounded in the project rather than invented.

`crimes context src/Card.tsx --format json` confirms all three findings, medium overall risk, and no related tests. I’d refactor the card into a responsive layout with a separate semantic action:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
  actionLabel: string;
};

export function Card({
  children,
  onActivate,
  actionLabel,
}: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="card">
      <div className="card__content">{children}</div>
      <aside className="card__sidebar">sidebar</aside>

      <button
        className="card__action"
        type="button"
        onClick={onActivate}
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

The accompanying stylesheet should use the project’s `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` tokens, plus named spacing, border, shadow, and typography tokens:

```css
.card {
  container-type: inline-size;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(10rem, 30%);
  width: 100%;
  max-width: var(--size-content-wide);
  min-height: var(--size-card-min);
  padding: var(--space-6);
  color: var(--color-ink);
  background: var(--color-surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.card__content {
  min-width: 0;
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.95rem + 0.25cqi, 1.125rem);
}

.card__sidebar {
  color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
}

.card__action {
  color: var(--color-brand);
}

@container (width < 36rem) {
  .card {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
}
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the non-semantic `<div>` and exposes the action as a native `<button>`, giving keyboard activation, focus behavior, and screen-reader semantics automatically. Keeping arbitrary `children` outside the button also avoids nesting links or controls inside another interactive element.
- `design_token_escape`: moves styling out of JSX and replaces the nine raw colors, pixel values, RGB shadow, and outline values with named design tokens. The existing `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` theme values should be the source of the corresponding CSS custom properties; missing border, shadow, spacing, and sizing tokens should be added centrally.
- `responsive_fragility`: replaces the fixed `800px` and `720px` widths with fluid grid tracks, `width: 100%`, and a tokenized maximum width. Typography becomes fluid with `clamp()`, while a container query collapses the sidebar without coupling the component to viewport-specific breakpoints.

Because `context` found no likely tests, I’d add a component test covering keyboard activation, the accessible button name, and narrow-container layout behavior before landing the refactor.
